{#
Rendered on the target at mode 0600. It carries NO credential -- this is the one
service on the platform whose secrets are all user-owned, and the file is 0600
anyway because every rendered .env here is and a mode that varies by service is
a mode somebody has to justify per service.

NEITHER ADMIN_TOKEN NOR DISABLE_ADMIN_TOKEN IS RENDERED HERE, AND NEITHER MAY
BE. Anything saved in /admin writes a config.json that outranks every variable in
this file from then on. services/vaultwarden/compose.yml carries the four
measured states; the short version is that DISABLE_ADMIN_TOKEN=true serves the
whole panel unauthenticated, a set ADMIN_TOKEN serves a login, and an empty
ADMIN_TOKEN happens to disable the panel on this pin while still logging a
plaintext-token notice -- a release's behaviour rather than a guarantee.

Rendering a value empty is the obvious way to make it optional under the
platform's `${VAR:?}` convention, which is exactly why tasks/deploy.yml sweeps
the deployed Compose files for all three keys rather than trusting this comment.
That sweep is what makes adding the line fail the converge; nothing reads this
file for it, because a variable no Compose file names reaches no container.
#}
TZ={{ nas_timezone }}
PLATFORM_CONTAINER_CPUSET={{ platform_effective_container_cpuset }}
PLATFORM_PROJECT_NAME={{ platform_project_name | default('') }}
NAS_UID={{ nas_uid }}
NAS_GID={{ nas_gid }}
VAULTWARDEN_DATA_PATH={{ vaultwarden_data_host_path }}
VAULTWARDEN_HOST_PORT={{ vaultwarden_port }}
VAULTWARDEN_DOMAIN={{ vaultwarden_domain }}
VAULTWARDEN_SIGNUPS_ALLOWED={{ vaultwarden_signups_allowed | bool | lower }}
VAULTWARDEN_INVITATIONS_ALLOWED={{ vaultwarden_invitations_allowed | bool | lower }}
